\maketitle

\noindent \textsc{Running Title:} Individual variation alters forecasts

\begin{abstract} \noindent
Mechanistic models are central to forecasting biological responses to climate change because they simulate organism--environment interactions that determine growth, survival and reproduction. Yet these models generally represent species by a single average phenotype, despite substantial physiological variation among individuals. Because organismal performance emerges from nonlinear interactions, Jensen's inequality predicts that forecasts for an average phenotype may differ systematically from those obtained by integrating across heterogeneous individuals. Whether the magnitude of this bias changes across environments and warming scenarios remains largely unknown. Here, we combined longitudinal measurements of growth and metabolism from individually monitored alpine newts (\emph{Ichthyosaura alpestris}) with Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) models to estimate physiological parameters for an average phenotype and for individual phenotypes. We coupled these parameterisations with biophysical models to predict twelve life-history and physiological performance traits across Europe under current climate and \qty{+2}{\celsius} and \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming scenarios. We then compared average-phenotype forecasts with forecasts integrating across individuals to quantify the bias arising from nonlinear physiological averaging. Average-phenotype forecasts consistently diverged from heterogeneous-population forecasts, but the magnitude and sign of this bias depended on the performance trait, geographic location and warming scenario. Reproductive traits were more sensitive to physiological averaging than structural traits, while warming amplified, attenuated or reversed bias depending on trait and environmental context. Nevertheless, both parameterisations recovered the same broad spatial pattern of biological responses to climate warming, but differed in the magnitude of the predicted changes. Our results identify biological heterogeneity as an important, context-dependent source of uncertainty in mechanistic climate forecasting. Representing populations as distributions of physiological phenotypes rather than single average individuals provides a means to propagate this heterogeneity through forecasts and to identify the traits, regions and climate scenarios for which predictions are most sensitive to nonlinear averaging.
\end{abstract}

{\small \noindent
  \textbf{Keywords:} biophysical modelling; climate change; Dynamic Energy Budget theory; intraspecific variation; Jensen's inequality; niche modelling
}

\section{Introduction}

Predicting how populations will respond to climate change remains one of the central challenges in ecology. Increasingly, these predictions rely on mechanistic approaches that simulate how organisms interact with their abiotic environment, thereby linking environmental conditions to organismal performance \parencite{kearney2009,kearney2017a,kearney2020c,briscoe2023}. By coupling organismal functional traits (e.g., morphology, physiology and behaviour) with microclimatic conditions derived from first principles, mechanistic models generate biologically grounded forecasts, overcoming some of the limitations of purely correlative approaches \parencite{kearney2009,urban2016,briscoe2023}, especially when environmental conditions extend beyond those currently experienced by species \parencite{elith2009,maino2016}. Mechanistic forecasts have therefore become increasingly realistic in their representation of environmental heterogeneity. Yet, organismal heterogeneity is rarely treated with the same resolution (but see \cite{enriquez-urzelai2020,kolbe2010,riddell2022}). How well this simplification captures population responses to environmental change remains largely unknown.

Individuals within populations frequently differ in morphology, physiology, behaviour and life-history traits, and these differences influence resource use, species interactions, demographic rates and ecosystem functioning \parencite{bolnick2003,bolnick2011,desroches2018}. Among the many dimensions of individual variation, physiology is particularly relevant for understanding responses to environmental change because it provides the mechanistic link between environmental conditions and ecological performance \parencite{seebacher2012}. Individuals commonly differ in metabolic rates, thermal sensitivities, or energy allocation and the plasticity of these traits \parencite{pettersen2024,gvozdik2024}. Consequently, environmental change acts on distributions of physiological phenotypes, whereas mechanistic forecasts commonly represent populations using a single average phenotype. Whether ignoring this variation biases forecasts, however, depends on how physiological variation interacts with environmental conditions to determine organismal performance.

\begin{figure}[!htb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/2_final/fig1-conceptual.pdf}
  \caption{Jensen's inequality and its implications for forecasting biological responses under climate change. For a nonlinear function the value at the mean $f(\overline{\theta})$ differs from the mean of the values $\overline{f(\theta)}$, producing a forecasting bias (hereafter Jensen bias, $J = f(\overline{\theta}) - \overline{f(\theta)}$), where $J \approx -\frac{1}{2} f''(\overline{\theta})\,\operatorname{Var}[\theta]$, and whose sign and magnitude follow the local curvature of $f$ \parencite{denny2017,jensen1906}.
  \textbf{(a) Representative trait--performance surface illustrating common forms of nonlinear responses to temperature}. The blue-to-red bar on the far left represents an environmental temperature gradient (cold to warm). Performance ($F$) is shown as a function of trait value ($\theta$) and temperature ($T$) for five illustrative types: concave dome, Gaussian, Gaussian with shifting thermal optimum ($T_{opt}$), sigmoidal with fixed threshold, and sigmoidal with shifting threshold. Coloured vertical slices show the trait--performance relationship at cold (\emph{blue}), intermediate (\emph{black}) and warm (\emph{red}) temperatures, while \emph{black points} denote the mean trait value. 
  \textbf{(b) Temperature-dependent Jensen bias predicted for each response surface}. \emph{Red shading} indicates positive bias ($J > 0$), where predictions based on the mean trait overestimate performance, whereas the \emph{blue shading} indicates negative bias ($J < 0$), where the mean trait underestimates performance. The \emph{solid curve} represents the bias under the current climate, $J(T)$, and the \emph{dashed curve} the bias after warming, $J(T+\Delta T)$, illustrating how climate change shifts the magnitude and sign of forecasting bias for a fixed trait distribution after warming ($\bar\theta$ and $\operatorname{Var}[\theta]$ held constant). 
  \textbf{(c) Impact of climate warming on the magnitude of Jensen bias}. The \emph{black curve} shows the change in absolute bias, $|J|$, across the temperature gradient. \emph{Yellow shading} denotes amplification ($|J|$ increases), whereas \emph{purple shading} denotes attenuation ($|J|$ decreases). Together, the examples demonstrate that the same warming scenario can either strengthen or weaken forecasting bias depending solely on the curvature and temperature dependence of the underlying trait--performance relationship.} 
  \label{fig:conceptual}
\end{figure}

The ecological consequences of consistent among-individual variation in physiological traits, however, cannot be understood from trait variation alone. Performance emerges from interactions between organismal phenotypes and environmental conditions. These interactions can be represented as multidimensional performance surfaces describing how a given performance trait varies across combinations of physiological phenotypes and abiotic conditions (\cite{laughlin2015,li2021}; \textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:conceptual}a}). Such surfaces are rarely linear. Instead, they commonly exhibit thermal optima, thresholds, asymmetries and other nonlinear features that are pervasive in ecological systems \parencite{ruel1999,bolnick2011}. Consequently, the ecological consequences of individual variation depend not only on the amount of physiological variation within a population, but also on the environmental context in which it is expressed: the same distribution of physiological phenotypes may produce similar performance in some environments but markedly different performance in others \parencite{ross2023}. Moreover, different components of performance are likely to be associated with different performance surfaces. Growth, reproduction or survival might not share the same thermal optima, thresholds or nonlinearities \parencite{sinclair2016,kontopoulos2024}, suggesting that the effects of physiological variation, and the biases introduced by physiological averaging, may differ among components of organismal performance.

These ideas have important consequences for ecological forecasting. When the relationship between phenotype, environment and performance is nonlinear, relying on an average phenotype for predictions yields different results than accounting for the phenotypic diversity present in real populations, a consequence of Jensen's inequality (\cite{ruel1999,jensen1906}; \textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:conceptual}a,b}). We define Jensen bias as $J = f(\overline{\theta}) - \overline{f(\theta)}$, i.e., the difference between performance predicted from the average phenotype and mean performance predicted across individual phenotypes. Importantly, $J$ does not arise from physiological variation alone, but from its interaction with the geometry of the underlying performance surface \parencite{denny2017,denny2019,laughlin2015}: where the local curvature is convex, predictions based on the average phenotype underestimate the average performance of heterogeneous populations ($J<0$), whereas where it is concave, they overestimate it ($J>0$; \cite{bolnick2011,ruel1999}). Different performance-surface geometries, as well as different regions within the same performance surface (e.g., along environmental gradients or across species' geographic ranges), therefore generate distinct patterns of averaging bias (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:conceptual}b}). Because climate change alters environmental conditions \parencite{simon2024} and thus where populations lie on the performance surface, Jensen bias may be amplified, attenuated or even reversed under warming (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:conceptual}c}).

Here, we combined longitudinal measurements of growth and metabolic rates from individually monitored alpine newts (\emph{Ichthyosaura alpestris}; data from \cite{baskiera2021}) with coupled Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) and biophysical models. This framework allowed us to evaluate how among-individual variation in physiological traits influences climate-change forecasts across the species' geographic distribution. DEB theory links environmental conditions to energy acquisition, allocation and expenditure throughout the life cycle \parencite{kooijman2010}, while biophysical models reconstruct the thermal and hydric environments experienced by organisms from local microclimates \parencite{kearney2013,kearney2009}. Coupling these approaches therefore allows empirically measured physiological variation to propagate through nonlinear phenotype--environment--performance relationships under current and future climates (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:conceptual}}). By comparing forecasts generated from population-average and individual-level physiological parameterisations under identical environmental simulations, we directly quantified the forecasting bias introduced by physiological averaging. We predicted that (1) forecasts based on an average physiological phenotype would diverge from those incorporating individual-level physiological variation where phenotype--environment--performance relationships were strongly nonlinear; (2) the magnitude and direction of this bias would differ among performance components and across environmental gradients because of differences in local performance-surface geometry; and (3) climate change would amplify, attenuate or reverse these biases by shifting populations into different regions of the performance surface. By linking empirical estimates of individual physiological variation to the geometry of trait--environment--performance surfaces, this study quantifies when and where physiological averaging matters most for ecological forecasts under climate change.

\section{Materials and Methods}

\subsection{Study system and data}

We used the alpine newt (\emph{I.~alpestris}) as a model species, a temperate amphibian widely distributed across Europe. Juveniles are strictly terrestrial: they usually leave their aquatic larval habitat and disperse on land until sexual maturity \parencite{denoel2004}. Adults spend most of the year on land except the breeding season (April--June). Terrestrial individuals are predominantly nocturnal or crepuscular and use a variety of daytime retreats, including leaf litter, stones, dead tree trunks and burrows of other animals \parencite{rocek2003}. Juveniles, as other predominantly nocturnal amphibians \parencite{enriquez-urzelai2020}, can show diurnal activity (especially after metamorphosis) to acquire energy reserves before their first winter. The preferred body temperature of the species is \qty{17.3}{\celsius}, with a lower bound of thermal preference of \qty{13.8}{\celsius} and an upper bound of \qty{20.0}{\celsius} \parencite{gvozdik2017}, although individuals remain capable of locomotion at temperatures approaching \qty{1}{\celsius} (Baškiera \& Gvoždík, unpublished data).

We compiled two complementary datasets describing the life history and physiological variation of the species. The first dataset consisted of species-level life-history and growth information compiled from the literature: these data included lifespan (20 years), snout--vent length at metamorphosis (\qty{1.83}{\cm}), female and male snout--vent length at sexual maturity (4.9 and \qty{4.4}{\cm}, respectively), asymptotic snout--vent length (\qty{5.84}{\cm} females, \qty{5.05}{\cm} males), asymptotic body mass (\qty{3.16}{\g} females, \qty{2.04}{\g} males), and maximum reproductive rate (\qty{600}{\eggs\per\year}) \parencite{gvozdik2017,miaud2000a}. These data were complemented with published growth (time--snout-vent-length) trajectories \parencite{miaud2000a}. The second dataset consisted of longitudinal measurements from 37 alpine newts (18 females and 19 males) from a population near Jihlava, Czech Republic (\ang{49.2247}\,N, \ang{16.0579}\,E; \qty{600}{m} a.s.l.). These individuals were monitored annually from 2014 (year of metamorphosis) to 2018. Each year, body mass (in \si{\gram}) and standard metabolic rate (in \si{\milli\liter\Otwo\per\hour}) were measured, providing growth and metabolic trajectories for each individual \parencite{baskiera2021}.

\subsection{DEB model parameter estimation}

Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) theory \parencite{sousa2008,kooijman2010} describes the acquisition, storage, and allocation of energy and mass throughout the life cycle. The standard DEB model \parencite{marques2018} represents an organism by three state variables: structural volume $V$, reserve energy $E$, and cumulative energy invested in maturation $E_H$ (plus a reproduction buffer $E_R$ in adults). Energy is assimilated into the reserve at a rate proportional to surface area and mobilised from it to fuel somatic maintenance, growth, maturity maintenance, and maturation (in juveniles) or reproduction (in adults), with life-history transitions occurring at maturity thresholds, e.g., birth at $E_H^b$ and sexual maturity at $E_H^p$ \parencite{kooijman2010,marques2018,oliveira2026}. The model is governed by 12 primary parameters \parencite{kooijman2010}. Among them, the zoom factor $z$ scales maximum body size and the maximum surface-specific assimilation rate $\{\dot{p}_{Am}\}$ \parencite{sousa2008}; reserves are mobilised at a rate set by the energy conductance $\dot{v}$; and, following the $\kappa$-rule, a fraction $\kappa$ of the mobilised reserve is allocated to somatic maintenance, at a cost $[\dot{p}_M]$ per unit of structural volume, and growth, while the remainder supports maturation or reproduction. Because each parameter quantifies the rate or efficiency of a specific physiological process, the model provides a coherent framework for interpreting individual variation in terms of energetic traits with distinct ecological meaning \parencite{kooijman2021}. A full description of the model and its parameters is given in \textbf{Appendix~\ref{appendix-methods}}.

Because DEB parameters describe latent energetic processes rather than directly measurable biological traits, they were estimated by inverse modelling using the covariation method \parencite{lika2011,marques2018}, with the AmPtool and DEBtool\_M packages in MATLAB (The MathWorks Inc.). Parameter estimation was performed at two levels: first, a single population-average parameterisation representing the average physiological phenotype (i.e., as is standard practice in mechanistic modelling, a single parameter set fitted to data pooled from multiple individuals; hereafter, average phenotype), and second, separate parameterisations for each of the 37 individuals. For the population-average parameterisation, the model was fitted simultaneously to the species-level life-history information and the complete set of longitudinal growth and metabolic observations from all 37 individuals, which were retained as separate data points rather than averaged across individuals or ages. We did not average the observations because averaging nonlinear growth and metabolic trajectories produces a composite trajectory that no individual actually follows, which would itself introduce the averaging bias examined here; fitting all observations simultaneously instead yields the parameter set that best describes the pooled population. Of the 12 primary parameters, we estimated seven ($z$, $\dot{v}$, $\kappa$, $[\dot{p}_M]$, the specific cost of structure $[E_G]$, and the maturity thresholds $E_H^b$ and $E_H^p$), together with a maturity threshold at metamorphosis and male-specific zoom factor ($z_m$) and puberty threshold ($E_{H,m}^p$) to account for sexual size dimorphism; the remaining parameters were fixed at standard values \parencite{kooijman2010,kearney2013}. Parameter values are given in \textbf{Table~\ref{tab:deb-params}}, and further details of the estimation and goodness of fit in \textbf{Appendix~\ref{appendix-methods}}.

Individual-level parameters (\textbf{Table~\ref{tab:deb-params}}) were obtained by re-estimating three parameters independently for each individual: the sex-specific zoom factor ($z$ for females, $z_m$ for males), energy conductance ($\dot{v}$), and volume-specific somatic maintenance ($[\dot{p}_M]$). We allowed these parameters to vary among individuals because they govern the processes captured by our individual-level data: variation in body size and energy uptake (via $z$ and $z_m$), in energy mobilisation (via $\dot{v}$), and in maintenance costs (via $[\dot{p}_M]$) jointly determine how individuals acquire and use energy, and thus how they grow and respire under a given environment \parencite{kooijman2021}. All remaining parameters, including $\kappa$, $[E_G]$, and the maturity thresholds, were held at their population-average values. The estimation used each individual's own longitudinal growth and metabolic trajectories as univariate data, with population-average estimates serving as starting values. All individuals were assigned the same temperature (\qty{12}{\celsius}) and food availability (scaled functional response $f = 1$) during estimation, so that differences among individual parameter sets reflect differences in growth and metabolism rather than in the conditions experienced. Each individual was assigned the sex-appropriate puberty threshold ($E_H^p$ for females, $E_{H,m}^p$ for males). Consequently, differences among individuals in these parameters can be interpreted as functionally coherent physiological variation.

\subsection{Mechanistic modelling framework}

To simulate organismal performance under current and future climates, we coupled biophysical and Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) models using the NicheMapR R package \parencite{kearney2017a,kearney2020c}. This framework uses biophysical models to compute hourly body temperature ($T_b$), activity windows, and foraging opportunities given available microclimates and the organism's morphological, physiological and behavioural traits \parencite{porter1973,kearney2018}. These hourly values drive the DEB model: $T_b$ is applied to all metabolic rates through the Arrhenius correction, and, since we held food availability constant through the simulations, the time available for foraging determines the amount of energy (food) assimilated, which, together with body temperatures, shapes how organisms grow, develop and reproduce \parencite{kearney2020c,kearney2012a}.

We reconstructed microclimatic conditions with NicheMapR's microclimate model, as implemented in the \texttt{micro\_global()} function \parencite{kearney2017a,kearney2014}. The model uses monthly climatological means from the global 10~arc-minute climatology of \textcite{new2002}, together with terrain and soil properties, to estimate hourly air temperature, radiation, wind speed and relative humidity at the animal's height, as well as soil temperature and moisture profiles \parencite{kearney2014,kearney2017a}. Microclimate simulations were run for 22 years with the moisture and snow routines enabled \parencite{kearney2018a,kearney2020b}, excluding the first two years as spin-up, which resulted in simulations of up to 20 years (i.e., the maximum known lifespan of the species). We ran the microclimate simulations under fully unshaded (0\% shade) and deep shade (90\% shade) conditions.

To estimate \textit{I.~alpestris} performance and simulate its life cycle, we used the \texttt{ectotherm()} function of NicheMapR \parencite{kearney2020c}, parameterised for a terrestrial alpine newt with 90\% of the skin surface acting as a free water exchanger \parencite{enriquez-urzelai2024}. Individuals were assumed to be nocturnal and crepuscular, but juveniles were also allowed diurnal activity to acquire energy reserves before their first winter \parencite{enriquez-urzelai2020}. Animals were active only when body temperature was within their voluntary thermal limits ($VT_{min}=\qty{4}{\celsius}$, $VT_{max}=\qty{20}{\celsius}$) and aboveground relative humidity was at least 65\% \parencite{bonnefond2020,giacometti2025}; otherwise, they retreated underground, selecting depths (\qty{2.5}{\cm} to \qty{2}{\m}) that kept them within their critical thermal limits ($CT_{min} = \qty{-2}{\celsius}$, $CT_{max} = \qty{36}{\celsius}$) \parencite{enriquez-urzelai2024}. When active, individuals could select shaded microhabitats (0--90\% shade) to stay close to their preferred body temperature of \qty{17.3}{\celsius} \parencite{gvozdik2017,enriquez-urzelai2019}. Females reproduced following the batch reproductive model \parencite{pecquerie2009}, producing clutches of 200--600 eggs, whereas in males the reproductive buffer was continuously depleted during the breeding season (between the spring equinox and the summer solstice). Simulations started at the juvenile stage on 15 August and ran until the individual died (see \textbf{Appendix~\ref{appendix-methods}} for further details).

Forecasts were generated for every terrestrial cell of a 10~arc-minute grid covering Europe (\ang{10}\,W--\ang{40}\,E, \ang{35}--\ang{70}\,N). For each grid cell, the coupled ectotherm--DEB model was run using the population-average parameterisation (female and male) and each of the 37 individual parameterisations (18 females and 19 males). The population-average and individual-level DEB parameter sets included the appropriate sex-specific zoom factors ($z$, $z_m$) and puberty thresholds ($E_H^p$, $E_{H,m}^p$).

To evaluate the thermal effects of climate change, the complete simulation framework was repeated under two warming scenarios corresponding to uniform increases of \qty{+2}{\celsius} and \qty{+4}{\celsius} in hourly air temperature relative to the current climate. These scenarios encompass the range of projected warming across Europe under intermediate to high emission pathways \parencite{calvin2023}. Warming was implemented as a uniform additive offset to the baseline climate's temperature forcing variable, whereas all other climatic variables (precipitation, relative humidity, wind speed, cloud cover and solar radiation) remained unchanged. This approach isolates the direct thermal effects of warming on ectotherm energetics while allowing direct comparison among climate scenarios. Note, however, that because relative humidity was held constant, warmer air implies a higher vapour pressure deficit and, therefore, higher evaporative water loss.

\subsection{Quantifying Jensen bias}

We quantified Jensen bias as the difference between the prediction obtained from the average phenotype (i.e., the population-average parameterisation) and the mean of the predictions obtained from the individual phenotypes (i.e., the individual-level parameterisations). For every grid cell, physiological parameterisation and climate scenario, we extracted 12 model outputs describing life-history and physiological performance: lifespan (\si{\year}), lifetime reproductive output (number of eggs in females, hereafter lifetime fecundity; reproductive energy, \si{\joule}, in males), net reproductive rate ($R_0$), maximum and minimum annual body temperatures ($T_{b,max}$, $T_{b,min}$), maximum body mass (\si{\g}), maximum snout--vent length (\si{\cm}), minimum reserve energy (\si{\joule}), cumulative basking time (\si{\hour}), cumulative foraging time (\si{\hour}), cumulative food intake (\si{\g}, dry mass) and mean daily water need (g~day$^{-1}$). Cumulative metrics were summed over each individual's entire simulated lifetime. Following NicheMapR terminology, basking time refers to the hours an individual spends above ground without foraging; for a predominantly nocturnal amphibian such as \emph{I.~alpestris}, it therefore represents surface exposure for thermoregulation rather than heliothermic basking \emph{sensu stricto}.

For each performance metric, we generated four prediction maps: two population-average predictions ($P_{\mathrm{pop}}$, for females and males) obtained from the population-average parameterisation, and two individual-level predictions obtained by averaging the outputs from the individual parameterisations ($\overline{P}_{\mathrm{ind}}$, for female and male individuals). Forecasting bias associated with physiological averaging was quantified for every grid cell as
\[
J=P_{\mathrm{pop}}-\overline{P}_{\mathrm{ind}}
\]
where $J$ measures the discrepancy between the population-average prediction and the mean of the individual-level predictions. Positive values of $J$ indicate that the population-average parameterisation overpredicts performance relative to the mean of individuals, whereas negative values indicate underprediction. We computed $J$ for females and males separately. Because performance metrics differ in their units, we compared the magnitude of bias among metrics using the normalised absolute Jensen bias, i.e., the mean $|J|$ across grid cells divided by the mean $|\overline{P}_{\mathrm{ind}}|$ across grid cells ($\times 100$).

To quantify spatial variation in the relative among-individual variability of predicted performance, independently of Jensen bias, we also calculated the coefficient of variation (CV) across the individual-level predictions of each sex (18 females and 19 males), for each metric and grid cell, and compared its median among southern ($<$\ang{40}\,N), central and northern ($>$\ang{60}\,N) latitudes. High CV values identify geographical regions and physiological metrics where individual differences translate into the most divergent predicted outcomes.

To evaluate how climate warming modified forecasting bias, we calculated the absolute Jensen bias ($|J|$) for each climate scenario and computed
\[
\Delta |J|=|J_{\mathrm{future}}|-|J_{\mathrm{current}}|
\]
Positive values indicate amplification of forecasting bias under warming, whereas negative values indicate attenuation. For each metric, we summarised the change in bias as the relative change in absolute Jensen bias (rel.\ $\Delta|J|$), i.e., the mean $\Delta|J|$ across grid cells divided by the mean $|J_{\mathrm{current}}|$ across grid cells ($\times 100$). We additionally quantified the proportion of grid cells in which $|J|$ increased, decreased or remained unchanged, as well as the proportion in which the sign of $J$ (overprediction versus underprediction) reversed between climate scenarios.

\subsection{Consequences of Jensen bias for forecasting}

To examine whether Jensen bias varied across environmental gradients, we related the change in absolute Jensen bias ($\Delta |J|$) under \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming to mean annual air temperature ($\overline{T}_a$), calculated for each grid cell from the climatology of \textcite{new2002}. For each metric and sex, $\Delta|J|$ was summarised as binned means in 1\,${}^{\circ}$C $\overline{T}_a$ intervals, and the overall trend was described with a generalised additive model (GAM) with a smooth term for $\overline{T}_a$ using the \texttt{mgcv} R package \parencite{R-mgcv}. Because model outputs are deterministic and neighbouring grid cells are spatially autocorrelated, GAMs were used as descriptive smoothers rather than for statistical inference (model specification in \textbf{Appendix~\ref{appendix-methods}}).

To evaluate the ecological implications of physiological averaging more directly, we compared forecasts generated from the population-average and individual-level physiological parameterisations for three representative life-history traits, summarising survival, growth and reproduction: lifespan, maximum body mass and lifetime fecundity (or reproductive energy for males). For each trait, grid cell and climate scenario, we compared the population-average prediction with the corresponding mean individual-level prediction, both under the current climate and as projected changes under warming ($P_{\mathrm{future}} - P_{\mathrm{current}}$). We report the percentage of grid cells in which the population-average model under- or overpredicted the individual-level mean, and the percentage in which both parameterisations projected the same direction of change. In addition, we computed the relative bias, $(P_{\mathrm{pop}} - \overline{P}_{\mathrm{ind}}) / |\overline{P}_{\mathrm{ind}}| \times 100$, where positive values indicate overestimation by the population-average model. Because relative bias is undefined or unstable where the individual-level mean is zero or close to zero, we summarised it across the species' range by its median and by the 95th percentile of its absolute value (i.e., the magnitude below which 95\% of grid-cell estimates fall).

\section{Results}

\subsection{Individual physiological variation}

Individual DEB parameters varied across the 37 individuals, with coefficients of variation (CV) spanning from \Sexpr{round(min(cv_sorted), 1)}\% ($z$) to \Sexpr{round(max(cv_sorted), 1)}\% ($[\dot{p}_M]$; \textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:zvpM-cv}}). Among the individually estimated parameters, the volume-specific somatic maintenance cost $[\dot{p}_M]$ was the most variable (CV = \Sexpr{round(cv_sorted["p_M"], 1)}\%), followed by energy conductance $\dot{v}$ (CV = \Sexpr{round(cv_sorted["v"], 1)}\%). The zoom factors ($z$: CV = \Sexpr{round(cv_sorted["z"], 1)}\%; $z_m$: CV = \Sexpr{round(cv_sorted["z_m"], 1)}\%) were less variable. The distribution of each parameter across the 37 individuals appeared approximately normal around the population-average estimate (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:zvpM-distribution}}). In addition, comparisons between observed and DEB-predicted growth trajectories (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:fit-growth}}) and metabolic rates (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:fit-metabolism}}) confirm that both population-average and individual-level parameterisations reproduced the empirical data accurately. Example 20-year DEB simulations for the population-average parameterisation (female and male) and selected individuals are shown in \textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:growth-1}--\ref{fig:growth-10}}.

\subsection{Physiological averaging generates Jensen bias}

\begin{figure}[!b]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/2_final/fig2-jensen-female.pdf}
  \caption{Jensen bias ($J$) between population-average and mean individual-level prediction surfaces for females under the current climate. Negative values indicate underprediction by the population-average parameterisation, whereas positive values indicate overprediction; values close to zero indicate close agreement. The colour scale is symmetric and centred at zero. The black outline delimits the current distribution of \emph{I.~alpestris} \parencite{iucn2021}. $T_b$, body temperature.}
  \label{fig:map-jensen}
\end{figure}

Under the current climate, predictions generated from the population-average DEB parameterisation differed systematically from the mean of the individual-level predictions for most performance metrics, although the direction and magnitude of these differences varied among metrics. For females, the population-average parameterisation underestimated most performance metrics relative to the individual-level mean (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-jensen}}), including basking time ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_F, metric == "Bsk")$mean_J_cur), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Bsk"]}), foraging time ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_F, metric == "Forage")$mean_J_cur), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Forage"]}), lifetime fecundity ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_F, metric == "Fecundity")$mean_J_cur), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Fecundity"]}), food intake ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_F, metric == "Food")$mean_J_cur), 1)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Food"]}) and maximum body mass ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_F, metric == "MaxWgt")$mean_J_cur), 1)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["MaxWgt"]}; here and below, reported values are the absolute value of the mean $J$ across grid cells). Relative to the individual-level mean, bias was largest for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_F_top)[1]]} (normalised $|J| = \Sexpr{norm_absJ_F_top[1]}$\%), \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_F_top)[2]]} (\Sexpr{norm_absJ_F_top[2]}\%) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_F_top)[3]]} (\Sexpr{norm_absJ_F_top[3]}\%), and smallest for body temperatures ($\leq \Sexpr{max(norm_absJ_F[c("Tmax", "Tmin")])}$\%). Only maximum and minimum body temperature showed a consistent positive bias, but with negligible magnitudes (mean $|J| < \Sexpr{ceiling(max(subset(stats_F, metric %in% c("Tmax", "Tmin"))$mean_absJ_cur) * 1000) / 1000}$\,${}^{\circ}$C). The pattern for males was largely opposite: the population-average parameterisation overestimated most metrics, including reproductive energy ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_M, metric == "ReproEnergy")$mean_J_cur_M), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["ReproEnergy"]}), basking time ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_M, metric == "Bsk")$mean_J_cur_M), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Bsk"]}) and foraging time ($|J| = \Sexpr{round(abs(subset(stats_M, metric == "Forage")$mean_J_cur_M), 0)}$\,\Sexpr{METRIC_UNITS["Forage"]}) (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-jensen-M}}). In relative terms, male bias was largest for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_M_top)[1]]} (normalised $|J| = \Sexpr{norm_absJ_M_top[1]}$\%), \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_M_top)[2]]} (\Sexpr{norm_absJ_M_top[2]}\%) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[names(norm_absJ_M_top)[3]]} (\Sexpr{norm_absJ_M_top[3]}\%).

The direction of Jensen bias differed among ecological performance metrics. Underestimation dominated across most of the geographic range for body size, reserve, food, lifetime fecundity and basking metrics ($J<0$ in \Sexpr{min(subset(stats_F, metric %in% c("MaxLen", "MaxWgt", "MinRes", "Food", "Fecundity", "Bsk"))$pct_neg)}--\Sexpr{max(subset(stats_F, metric %in% c("MaxLen", "MaxWgt", "MinRes", "Food", "Fecundity", "Bsk"))$pct_neg)}\% of cells), whereas maximum and minimum body temperature showed the opposite pattern, with predominantly positive bias ($J>0$ in \Sexpr{min(subset(stats_F, metric %in% c("Tmax", "Tmin"))$pct_pos)}--\Sexpr{max(subset(stats_F, metric %in% c("Tmax", "Tmin"))$pct_pos)}\% of cells). Foraging time showed a more balanced distribution (\Sexpr{subset(stats_F, metric == "Forage")$pct_pos}\% positive, \Sexpr{subset(stats_F, metric == "Forage")$pct_neg}\% negative and \Sexpr{100 - subset(stats_F, metric == "Forage")$pct_pos - subset(stats_F, metric == "Forage")$pct_neg}\% with no bias). For males, the pattern was reversed: overestimation dominated for body size, reserve, food, reproductive energy and basking metrics ($J>0$ in \Sexpr{min(subset(stats_M, metric %in% c("MaxLen", "MaxWgt", "MinRes", "Food", "ReproEnergy", "Bsk"))$pct_pos_M)}--\Sexpr{max(subset(stats_M, metric %in% c("MaxLen", "MaxWgt", "MinRes", "Food", "ReproEnergy", "Bsk"))$pct_pos_M)}\% of cells), whereas maximum and minimum body temperature showed predominantly negative bias ($J<0$ in \Sexpr{min(subset(stats_M, metric %in% c("Tmax", "Tmin"))$pct_neg_M)}--\Sexpr{max(subset(stats_M, metric %in% c("Tmax", "Tmin"))$pct_neg_M)}\% of cells).

The magnitude of physiological heterogeneity also varied geographically (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-cv}, \ref{fig:map-cv-male}}). Compared with central latitudes (\ang{40}--\ang{60}\,N), the median coefficient of variation among individual-level predictions was higher towards the southern ($<$\ang{40}\,N) or northern ($>$\ang{60}\,N) margins of the modelled area, but the strength of this pattern differed markedly among metrics and between sexes. In females, the median CV at the southern margin was \Sexpr{cv_ratio("Fecundity", "F")}-fold higher than at central latitudes for lifetime fecundity, \Sexpr{cv_ratio("MinRes", "F")}-fold for minimum reserve and \Sexpr{cv_ratio("MaxLen", "F")}-fold for maximum body length. In males, the largest increases occurred at the northern margin for $R_0$ (\Sexpr{cv_ratio("R0", "M")}-fold) and reproductive energy (\Sexpr{cv_ratio("ReproEnergy", "M")}-fold), and at the southern margin for minimum reserve (\Sexpr{cv_ratio("MinRes", "M")}-fold). In contrast, lifespan, basking time, foraging time and body temperatures differed by at most \Sexpr{round(max(subset(cv_bands, metric %in% c("LifeSpan", "Bsk", "Forage", "Tmax", "Tmin"))$ratio), 1)}-fold between margins and central latitudes in either sex. Thus, the geographic pattern was not universal, but was mainly driven by reproduction- and reserve-related metrics and, in females, by growth-related metrics.

\subsection{Climate warming reshapes Jensen bias}

Climate warming altered the magnitude of Jensen bias for all performance metrics, but the direction and magnitude of these changes varied among metrics and between sexes (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-jensen-warming}, \ref{fig:map-jensen-warming-M}}).

\begin{figure}[!b]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/2_final/fig3-warming-female.pdf}
   \caption{Jensen bias amplification under \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming. Each map displays the change in absolute Jensen bias ($\Delta|J|$, left, with colour bar) for each performance metric. The curve (descriptive GAM smoother) at the right of each map represents how $\Delta|J|$ changes along the mean annual temperature ($\overline{T}_a$) gradient. Positive values (\textit{yellow}) indicate that warming increases the disagreement between population-average and mean individual-level predictions (amplification), whereas negative values (\textit{purple}) indicate reduced disagreement (attenuation). The black outline delimits the current distribution of \emph{I.~alpestris} \parencite{iucn2021}. $T_b$, body temperature.}
  \label{fig:map-jensen-warming}
\end{figure}

Under \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-jensen-warming}}), the absolute Jensen bias $|J|$ changed for all \Sexpr{nrow(stats_F)} metrics: the strongest relative amplification occurred for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[amp_w4$metric[1]]} (rel.\ $\Delta|J| = +$\Sexpr{round(amp_w4$rel_deltaJ_w4[1], 0)}\%; \Sexpr{round(amp_w4$pct_increase_w4[1], 0)}\% of cells) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[amp_w4$metric[2]]} (rel.\ $\Delta|J| = +$\Sexpr{round(amp_w4$rel_deltaJ_w4[2], 0)}\%; \Sexpr{round(amp_w4$pct_increase_w4[2], 0)}\% of cells), followed by \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[amp_w4$metric[3]]} (rel.\ $\Delta|J| = +$\Sexpr{round(amp_w4$rel_deltaJ_w4[3], 0)}\%); in contrast, \Sexpr{att_text_w4} were attenuated. Note, however, that the large relative changes in body temperature bias arise from very small absolute biases: mean $|J|$ increased from \Sexpr{signif(subset(stats_F, metric == "Tmin")$mean_absJ_cur, 2)} to \Sexpr{signif(subset(stats_F, metric == "Tmin")$mean_absJ_w4, 2)}\,${}^{\circ}$C for minimum and from \Sexpr{signif(subset(stats_F, metric == "Tmax")$mean_absJ_cur, 2)} to \Sexpr{signif(subset(stats_F, metric == "Tmax")$mean_absJ_w4, 2)}\,${}^{\circ}$C for maximum body temperature. For males, the strongest amplification occurred for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[amp_w4_M$metric[1]]} (rel.\ $\Delta|J| = +$\Sexpr{round(amp_w4_M$rel_deltaJ_w4[1], 0)}\%; \Sexpr{round(amp_w4_M$pct_increase_w4[1], 0)}\% of cells) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[amp_w4_M$metric[2]]} (rel.\ $\Delta|J| = +$\Sexpr{round(amp_w4_M$rel_deltaJ_w4[2], 0)}\%; \Sexpr{round(amp_w4_M$pct_increase_w4[2], 0)}\% of cells), whereas \Sexpr{att_text_w4_M} was attenuated.

Warming also reversed the direction of Jensen bias in some regions, causing population-average projections to switch between overestimating and underestimating mean individual performance. The sign of $J$ changed in \Sexpr{round(min(stats_F$pct_sign_change_w4), 0)}--\Sexpr{round(max(stats_F$pct_sign_change_w4), 0)}\% of cells between current and \qty{+4}{\celsius} scenarios, with highest rates for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign$metric[1]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign$pct_sign_change_w4[1], 0)}\%), \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign$metric[2]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign$pct_sign_change_w4[2], 0)}\%) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign$metric[3]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign$pct_sign_change_w4[3], 0)}\%). The spatial maps revealed geographically structured changes in Jensen bias, which were reflected in systematic relationships between $\Delta|J|$ and mean annual temperature. However, both the shape and direction of these temperature relationships differed among ecological performance metrics (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-jensen-warming}}). For males, the sign of $J$ changed in \Sexpr{round(min(stats_M$pct_sign_change_w4), 0)}--\Sexpr{round(max(stats_M$pct_sign_change_w4), 0)}\% of cells, with highest rates for \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign_M$metric[1]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign_M$pct_sign_change_w4[1], 0)}\%), \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign_M$metric[2]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign_M$pct_sign_change_w4[2], 0)}\%) and \Sexpr{METRIC_NAMES[top_sign_M$metric[3]]} (\Sexpr{round(top_sign_M$pct_sign_change_w4[3], 0)}\%).

Even under \qty{+2}{\celsius} warming, the direction of change was already discernible for most metrics. \Sexpr{nrow(amp_w2)} metrics showed increased $|J|$, with relative changes ranging from $+$\Sexpr{amp_w2_min_val}\% (\Sexpr{amp_w2_min_name}) to $+$\Sexpr{amp_w2_max_val}\% (\Sexpr{amp_w2_max_name}), while \Sexpr{nrow(att_w2)} metric was attenuated: \Sexpr{att_text_w2}. \Sexpr{nrow(switchers)} metric\Sexpr{ifelse(nrow(switchers) != 1, "s", "")} (\Sexpr{switchers_text}) switched from amplification at \qty{+2}{\celsius} to attenuation at \qty{+4}{\celsius}, suggesting a nonlinear threshold above which further warming attenuates rather than amplifies the averaging bias for this trait. For males, \Sexpr{nrow(amp_w2_M)} metrics showed increased $|J|$, with relative changes ranging from $+$\Sexpr{amp_w2_min_val_M}\% (\Sexpr{amp_w2_min_name_M}) to $+$\Sexpr{amp_w2_max_val_M}\% (\Sexpr{amp_w2_max_name_M}), while \Sexpr{nrow(att_w2_M)} metric was attenuated: \Sexpr{att_text_w2_M}. \Sexpr{nrow(switchers_M)} metric\Sexpr{ifelse(nrow(switchers_M) != 1, "s", "")} (\Sexpr{switchers_text_M}) switched from amplification at +2\,${}^{\circ}$C to attenuation at +4\,${}^{\circ}$C.

\subsection{Consequences for ecological forecasts}

\begin{figure}[!t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/2_final/fig4-species-vs-ind-female.pdf}
  \caption{Population-average and mean individual-level predictions and projections of lifespan, maximum body mass and lifetime fecundity under the current climate and \qty{+2}{\celsius} and \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming scenarios. For each trait, the top row shows absolute predictions from the population-average parameterisation and the mean of the individual-level parameterisations under each climate scenario, with all six maps displayed on a common trait-specific scale (\textit{green}). The bottom row shows the projected change under \qty{+2}{\celsius} and \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming relative to the corresponding current-climate prediction ($P_\mathrm{future} - P_\mathrm{current}$). For visualisation, projected changes were normalised within each trait by the maximum absolute change across all grid cells, parameterisation levels, and warming scenarios, yielding a common scale from -1 to 1. Negative values (\textit{red}) indicate decreases under warming, positive values (\textit{blue}) indicate increases, and values near zero indicate little change. The black outline delimits the current distribution of \emph{I.~alpestris} \parencite{iucn2021}.}
  \label{fig:map-suitability}
\end{figure}

The consequences of physiological averaging for ecological forecasts varied among life-history traits and climate scenarios (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-suitability}}). The population-average parameterisation systematically underestimated female performance and overestimated male performance for several traits, with differences in both the magnitude and geographical extent of bias. For females, underprediction of lifetime fecundity became more widespread with warming, increasing from \Sexpr{cq("F", "Fecundity", "cur", "pct_under")}\% of grid cells under the current climate (median relative bias $\Sexpr{cq("F", "Fecundity", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%) to \Sexpr{cq("F", "Fecundity", "w2", "pct_under")}\% and \Sexpr{cq("F", "Fecundity", "w4", "pct_under")}\% of cells under \qty{+2}{\celsius} and \qty{+4}{\celsius}, respectively. Similarly, the proportion of cells with underpredicted lifespan increased from \Sexpr{cq("F", "LifeSpan", "cur", "pct_under")}\% under the current climate (median relative bias $\Sexpr{cq("F", "LifeSpan", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%) to \Sexpr{cq("F", "LifeSpan", "w4", "pct_under")}\% under \qty{+4}{\celsius}. Maximum body mass was underpredicted in \Sexpr{cq("F", "MaxWgt", "cur", "pct_under")}\% of cells (median relative bias $\Sexpr{cq("F", "MaxWgt", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%). Males showed the opposite direction of bias (\textbf{Fig.~\ref{fig:map-suitability-M}}): the population-average model overpredicted reproductive energy in \Sexpr{cq("M", "ReproEnergy", "cur", "pct_over")}\% of cells under the current climate (median relative bias $+\Sexpr{cq("M", "ReproEnergy", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%), increasing to \Sexpr{cq("M", "ReproEnergy", "w4", "pct_over")}\% of cells under \qty{+4}{\celsius} (median $+\Sexpr{cq("M", "ReproEnergy", "w4", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%), and also overpredicted lifespan (\Sexpr{cq("M", "LifeSpan", "cur", "pct_over")}\% of cells; median $+\Sexpr{cq("M", "LifeSpan", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%) and maximum body mass (\Sexpr{cq("M", "MaxWgt", "cur", "pct_over")}\% of cells; median $+\Sexpr{cq("M", "MaxWgt", "cur", "med_rel_bias", 1)}$\%). Despite these differences, both parameterisations projected the same direction of change under \qty{+4}{\celsius} warming in at least \Sexpr{min_agree_w4}\% of grid cells for all three traits in both sexes.

The relative bias maps (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:pct-diff-F}, \ref{fig:pct-diff-M}}) revealed that, for females, the population-average model underestimated lifespan by up to \Sexpr{subset(pct95_rel_bias_F, metric == "LifeSpan")$pct95}\% and lifetime fecundity by up to \Sexpr{subset(pct95_rel_bias_F, metric == "Fecundity")$pct95}\% of the individual-level mean, whereas maximum body mass showed relative biases within $\pm$\Sexpr{subset(pct95_rel_bias_F, metric == "MaxWgt")$pct95}\% for 95\% of cells. Under warming, the spatial pattern and magnitude of relative bias shifted, with some regions showing reversed sign. For males, the population-average model overestimated reproductive energy by up to \Sexpr{subset(pct95_rel_bias_M, metric == "ReproEnergy")$pct95}\% of the individual-level mean, whereas lifespan and maximum body mass showed smaller relative biases.

\section{Discussion}

Mechanistic models have become central to forecasting biological responses to climate change because they explicitly link abiotic environmental conditions to organismal performance through physiology \parencite{fenollosa2025,pincebourde2020,puig2026}. Yet, in most cases species are represented by a single average phenotype. Our results show that this simplification introduces systematic biases that differ among ecological traits, across geographical space and under climate warming. These discrepancies arise because averaging physiological parameters before nonlinear organism--environment interactions does not necessarily yield the same predictions as averaging individual performance afterwards, consistent with Jensen's inequality \parencite{bolnick2011,ruel1999}. Extending previous studies on nonlinear responses to environmental variability \parencite{paaijmans2013,denny2017}, we show that variation in only three ecophysiological traits can generate systematic differences in continental-scale mechanistic forecasts. Rather than diminishing the value of mechanistic models, these findings demonstrate how incorporating among-individual physiological variation can help identify where predictions based on average phenotypes are robust and where they are susceptible to bias.

Among-individual physiological variation generated systematic, directional bias whose magnitude and, in some cases, even its sign depended on the ecological trait, sex and environmental context (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-jensen}, \ref{fig:map-jensen-M}}) \parencite{ruel1999,bolnick2011,denny2019}. In both sexes, reproductive output showed the largest relative bias, whereas the direction of bias was largely opposite between females (predominantly underestimation) and males (predominantly overestimation). Foraging time occupied an intermediate position, repeatedly shifting between over- and underestimation across the species' range, indicating that even the direction of Jensen bias is determined by local environmental conditions rather than by the trait itself \parencite{martin2008}. Together, these patterns indicate that traits integrating cumulative energy allocation over the life cycle, particularly reproduction, are considerably more sensitive to physiological averaging than traits dominated by structural growth, such as body mass \parencite{nisbet2012,palmer2024}. Reproductive output integrates energy acquisition, allocation and maturation history throughout adulthood \parencite{kooijman2010,jusup2017}, allowing relatively small physiological differences to accumulate through time, whereas body mass is constrained by tighter feedbacks between energy acquisition and maintenance \parencite{nisbet2012,lika2019} that might buffer the consequences of individual variation. The opposite direction of bias observed in females and males may partly arise from their contrasting reproductive allocation strategies, with batch reproduction amplifying energetic differences among females and continuous depletion of the reproductive buffer moderating them in males \parencite{muller2019,pecquerie2009}.

Physiological heterogeneity also had geographically structured consequences, indicating that the importance of individual variation depends on where populations occur within environmental space. Variation among the individual-level predictions was generally highest towards the northernmost and southernmost latitudes represented in the simulations, particularly for reproduction- and reserve-related metrics (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-cv}, \ref{fig:map-cv-male}}), showing that comparatively small physiological differences among individuals became increasingly important under environmentally constraining conditions. This result is consistent with theoretical predictions that the ecological consequences of trait variation emerge from the interaction between the amount of variation present and the local geometry of the underlying performance surface, which changes across environmental gradients \parencite{ross2023}. In other words, the same degree of physiological variation can have relatively small ecological consequences in some environments but substantially larger ones in others \parencite{li2021,laughlin2015}, depending on where populations operate on the performance surface \parencite{polazzo2024}. Although the strongest effects occurred beyond parts of the current realised distribution of \emph{I.~alpestris} \parencite{iucn2021}, they emerged under the most environmentally challenging conditions represented in the simulations, consistent with the expectation that ecological responses become increasingly sensitive as populations approach environmental limits \parencite{holt2003,sexton2009,valladares2014}. This pattern suggests that forecasts based on population-average physiology are therefore most likely to diverge from forecasts that explicitly represent individual variation precisely in situations where predicting persistence, range expansion and local extinction is most relevant \parencite{demarche2019}.

Furthermore, climate warming altered not only ecological performance itself, but also the magnitude and, in some cases, even the direction of the bias introduced by physiological averaging (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-jensen-warming}, \ref{fig:map-jensen-warming-M}}). Climate warming changed Jensen bias for all twelve ecological performance metrics in both sexes, but these changes were strongly trait dependent rather than uniform: in both sexes, warming amplified the bias in net reproductive rate, the most ecologically relevant amplification, whereas it attenuated the bias in lifespan (and, in females, in minimum reserve energy). Body temperatures showed the largest relative amplification, although their biases remained negligible in absolute terms. Warming also reversed the sign of Jensen bias across large parts of the species' range for some metrics, showing that whether population-average parameterisations over- or underestimate ecological performance depends on the environmental conditions under which populations are evaluated. Moreover, whereas most metrics already showed increased bias under \qty{+2}{\celsius}, lifespan switched from amplification at \qty{+2}{\celsius} to attenuation at \qty{+4}{\celsius}, demonstrating that the influence of physiological heterogeneity on ecological forecasts does not change monotonically with warming. Instead, warming can shift populations across different regions of nonlinear performance surfaces \parencite{angillettajr.2009,kingsolver2016,li2021}, such that further temperature increases weaken rather than strengthen the effects of physiological averaging. These results are consistent with the broader view that biological responses to climate change emerge from nonlinear interactions between organismal traits and environmental conditions, so that warming changes where populations operate on underlying performance surfaces rather than simply scaling biological responses \parencite{buckley2018,buckley2023,cannistra2024}. Our results show that these same nonlinearities also determine where forecasts based on average physiology provide robust predictions and where they systematically diverge from forecasts that explicitly account for physiological heterogeneity.

Despite the systematic biases introduced by physiological averaging, both physiological parameterisations converged on the same broad biological response of \emph{I.~alpestris} to climate warming, projecting the same direction of change in at least \Sexpr{min_agree_w4}\% of grid cells for lifespan, body mass and reproduction (\textbf{Figs.~\ref{fig:map-suitability}, \ref{fig:map-suitability-M}}). Under both parameterisations, warming reduced projected lifespan across most of the species' distribution, consistent with the faster life histories and higher cumulative metabolic costs expected for ectotherms under warmer conditions \parencite{munch2009,burraco2020}. At the same time, both approaches projected reduced body mass and reproductive output towards the north-eastern limit of the species' range, where climatic warming is expected to increase thermal suitability. This combination suggests that improved thermal conditions alone may not translate directly into greater demographic potential if gains in climatic suitability are accompanied by reductions in growth and reproductive performance \parencite{schulte2015,csergo2017}. Thus, incorporating individual physiological variation did not fundamentally alter the biological interpretation of the species' response to climate change, but instead revealed where and by how much forecasts became sensitive to the simplifying assumption of representing populations by a single physiological phenotype. Because mechanistic models explicitly represent organismal physiology, they are particularly well suited to incorporate intraspecific physiological variation, phenotypic plasticity and evolutionary responses directly into ecological predictions \parencite{riddell2023a}. Previous studies have propagated observed or hypothetical physiological variation through mechanistic models to investigate trait evolution, adaptive significance and thermal adaptation (e.g., \cite{dejong1996,riddell2022,kingsolver2016,enriquez-urzelai2020}); our results show that the same framework can also quantify where the robustness of mechanistic forecasts changes across ecological traits, geographic space and future climates.

As individual-level physiological datasets become increasingly available \parencite{briscoe2015,gardner2019,fyllas2020,albert2010}, propagating trait distributions offers a practical way to quantify the consequences of biological heterogeneity alongside the increasingly realistic representation of environmental heterogeneity. Realising this potential, however, depends on the availability of empirical data describing physiological variation among individuals. Physiological studies commonly report population or treatment means, often accompanied only by summary measures of dispersion, whereas the individual-level estimates on which those summaries are based are rarely reported \parencite{wolf2023}. Yet, it is precisely the distribution of individual phenotypes, rather than its central tendency, that determines whether and how strongly forecasts based on average phenotypes are biased. Thus, we strongly encourage empiricists to report individual-level estimates (or at least their full distributions and covariances) alongside population means, ideally in open repositories. Such data would enable the incorporation of realistic trait distributions into mechanistic models, allowing us to assess the robustness of ecological forecasts to physiological variation and to identify the environmental conditions and geographical regions where physiological heterogeneity has the greatest consequences for predicted responses to climate change.

Several limitations also point to important directions for future work. We only estimated individual variation for three DEB parameters per individual (the sex-specific zoom factor, $\dot{v}$ and $[\dot{p}_M]$). Variation in the remaining ecophysiological traits or in other traits related to morphology, thermal biology and behaviour could further modify both the magnitude and the spatial pattern of Jensen bias. Likewise, all individuals originated from a single population, whereas physiological trait distributions are likely to vary across species' ranges due to local adaptation, developmental history and long-term acclimation \parencite{moran2016,sexton2009,enriquez-urzelai2020}. Incorporating such among-population variation could amplify, attenuate or even reverse the spatial patterns of Jensen bias reported here. Finally, we assumed that physiological differences remained fixed under future climates, whereas phenotypic plasticity and evolutionary responses are expected to reshape trait distributions over the timescales relevant to climate change \parencite{chevin2010,kingsolver2017,riddell2023a}. Extending mechanistic forecasts to account simultaneously for individual variation, geographic differentiation and the dynamic reshaping of trait distributions through plasticity and adaptation therefore represents an important next step towards biologically realistic predictions under environmental change.

\section{Conclusion}

Our study demonstrates that representing populations by a single average physiological phenotype can introduce systematic, spatially structured biases that depend on the performance metric, sex and environmental conditions, and that are further modified by climate warming. Nevertheless, population-average and individual-level parameterisations generally agreed on the direction of the projected climate response, indicating that physiological averaging can affect quantitative predictions without necessarily changing their qualitative interpretation. These findings highlight an important distinction between the robustness of predicted trends and the accuracy of predicted performance. Incorporating empirically measured physiological variation into mechanistic models therefore offers a means of identifying when average-phenotype assumptions are adequate and when they may obscure ecologically relevant differences. More broadly, accounting for biological heterogeneity alongside environmental heterogeneity can improve our understanding of the conditions under which mechanistic climate forecasts are sensitive to physiological averaging.

